Lifetime: Static variables are initialized only once, and their values persist between function calls. They are not destroyed when the function in which they are declared exits.

Scope: The scope of a static variable depends on where it is declared. If declared inside a function, it has internal linkage and is only accessible within that function. If declared at the file level (outside of any function), it has internal linkage and is only accessible within the same translation unit (i.e., the same source file).

Initialization: Static variables are initialized to zero (for global/static variables) or to their specified initial value (for static local variables) if no explicit initialization is provided.

Usage: Static variables are useful when you need to maintain state between function calls. For example, they can be used to count the number of times a function is called.

